{
  "task": "yoga-studio",
  "_doc": "S for yoga-studio (greenfield: a paid booking web app, owner manages the schedule). The build's routes are unknown, so the probes are generic and FUNCTIONAL. (1) schedule-served: the schedule is served as real JSON records. (2) owner-action-works: with owner credentials the probe injects through OWNER_/ADMIN_ environment variables (or a common default), an authenticated request adds a class that then appears in the schedule, the same request without credentials is refused, and the login rejects a wrong password. (3) buyer-flow-works: either customer accounts (register, sign in, a session-gated account route that returns the customer's identity, wrong passwords rejected, owner routes refused to the customer) or guest checkout (two bookings without an account; an anonymous caller can then neither read nor alter anyone's booking). Every other probe (anonymous owner actions, XSS, SQL error leakage, command execution, path traversal, served-file leaks, error leakage, restart persistence) is credited only when those three controls were verified: a constant page, or a stub that answers 401 everywhere, proves nothing. Served-file leaks: every file of the build is requested; the probe customers' email, phone and session token (any file) and emails, phone numbers, card-like numbers and tokens (data files) must not be served. Persistence: the server is stopped and started a second time on the same data; the owner signs in again (wrong password still rejected), the owner's class is still in the schedule, and with accounts the probe customer signs in and the account route returns their own email. Limitation: the owner login must be reachable with the OWNER_/ADMIN_ variables above or a documented default; a build that hides its owner credentials cannot be probed and scores low on S. Cross-user checks with fixed routes belong to reserve tasks (http_crossuser).",
